WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:04.371
So Maggie,
if the if the National Archives are right,

00:00:04.371 --> 00:00:06.840
if they have proof that
that he was informed

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.143
of the correct procedure,
what does that say about his exposure?

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:13.847
Or if there's documentation
showing that Trump was aware

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:17.784
and the Trump aides were aware of what
actual declassification processes were?

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:20.186
That completely undercuts
what their claim has been

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:23.957
which is that he had a standing order
to automatically declassify all documents

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:27.360
when they left the Oval Office
and went up to the White House residence.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.330
Now. I don't think that he's
going to back away from that.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.998
We do know the special counsel's office

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:35.835
has been drilling down very, very hard on
exactly what the processes were.

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:39.439
They've interviewed a ton of people who
worked in the White House and would know.

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:41.641
But I think this is the first thing
that I've heard of

00:00:41.808 --> 00:00:44.811
that's actual documentation showing
what was what.

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:49.182
Because he keeps saying it was
a negotiation and that's what is expected.

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:51.451
That it's expected to be negotiated,
which isn't true.

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:52.385
And in fact, he said that

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:55.021
under the Presidential Records Act,
it says that it's a negotiation.

00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:57.891
And that's just not
what the Presidential Records Act says.

00:00:57.924 --> 00:01:00.060
Now, that's a separate issue
than the classification issue.

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:03.930
The classification issue is very specific
to intelligence material.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.398
And he has made all kinds of claims

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:08.701
that also have been debunked
by former officials working for him.

00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:11.738
But in both cases,
officials disagree with him.

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:12.572
Is it clear to you

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:16.009
how concerned he is about this
and how concerned the people around him on

00:01:16.443 --> 00:01:19.612
this is just another break in
what Jack Smith is looking for

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:22.382
that has made them
anxious overall about this case.

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:24.384
They are worried about the documents case.

00:01:24.384 --> 00:01:27.287
They believe the Jack Smith
is drilling down on this in a way that is

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.423
discomforting for Trump, discomforting
for a lot of people around him.

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:33.093
And this is just another piece.

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:35.462
What about the attorney who left?
Who was he?

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:36.463
How important?

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:40.233
So Tim Pawlenty was an attorney who had
actually been pretty central in the effort

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:43.603
to look for additional documents
around Trump's various properties.

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:45.605
He found himself
having to speak to the grand

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:48.641
jury as the custodian
of the records at that point.

00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:52.545
He also made a lot of statements in public
that were controversial

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:56.282
about the documents, including saying
I think it was on CNN that, you know,

00:01:56.316 --> 00:02:01.020
Trump had had a classified folder
on a bedside table in his residence.

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:05.158
The judge in the case,
I think, took note of those comments.

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:06.426
And it does raise questions

00:02:06.426 --> 00:02:09.662
about how if there was literally
a classified document in Trump's bedroom

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:11.965
that he was unaware
that he still had material.

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:12.632
Do you think?

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:15.401
I mean, obviously,
the former president's supporters

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:18.605
rallied around him
after the Stormy Daniels hush money case.

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:23.676
Is there any concern in Trumpworld that
the reaction to this might be different?

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:27.447
There is not that much concern
expressed by Trump folks.

00:02:27.447 --> 00:02:30.717
That people around him
and his base will see it differently.

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:33.486
That is the hope of some of his critics
and opponents,

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:36.556
which is that there will just be such
a fact set around this

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:39.993
that it will be distinct enough
to make the case that this is different.

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:41.094
But no, they think

00:02:41.094 --> 00:02:44.097
they're going to be able to paint this
and a potential indictment out of Georgia

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:47.567
in connection with his efforts
to remain in power in 2021,

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:51.471
that this is all going to be part
of what he calls the witch hunt

00:02:51.471 --> 00:02:53.406
and that his voters
won't see it differently.

00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:56.342
How organized is his campaign structure
at this point?

00:02:56.342 --> 00:02:58.211
You know, it's pretty organized, actually.

00:02:58.211 --> 00:03:00.980
I mean, there is a
there is a clear team of people.

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:02.949
They have their assigned roles.

00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:06.219
It doesn't mean that
they are going to be flawless

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:09.889
but, you know,
this is actually the most organized.

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:14.027
One of his campaigns in my memory
has been at least at least in this way

00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:15.328
at this stage in 20,

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:19.699
ten, 20, 20, It was it was well-organized
in various ways at various points.

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:23.136
But this early, this is more organized
than I remember it being.

00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:24.904
Maggie Haberman, thanks. Thank you.

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:26.673
The National Archives is set to hand over

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:29.976
16 presidential records to special counsel
Jack Smith

00:03:30.677 --> 00:03:34.414
for his investigation into Trump's
mishandling of classified documents.

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:38.651
It says they show that Trump and his top
advisers were aware of the proper

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:43.223
declassification process, that despite
Trump claiming multiple times,

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:47.627
he did declassify the papers simply
by removing them from the White House

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:50.897
If you're the president

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:54.667
of the United States, you can declassify
just by saying it's declassified.

00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:58.838
Even by thinking about it,
I have the right to declassify documents

00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:02.108
and the process is automatic.

00:04:02.108 --> 00:04:04.310
If I take them with me, it's automatic.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.313
By the way,
they become automatically declassified.

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:09.616
When I took them behind.

00:04:09.616 --> 00:04:11.150
Let me ask you.

00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:14.220
But they don't become automatically
declassified.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.022
We've heard that from his own advisers.

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:19.592
The 16 Records, which we are
now told was subpoenaed earlier this year.

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:23.896
Are going to be handed over next Wednesday
to Jack Smith and his investigation.

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:27.867
They could potentially help
those investigators overcome a significant

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.170
obstacle to a potential prosecution
of the former president.

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:34.007
So let's get legal insight
into all of that.

00:04:34.007 --> 00:04:36.109
Let's turn to CNN senior legal analyst
Ellie Hoenig.

00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:38.945
Big picture,
how much does this matter for prosecutors?

00:04:38.978 --> 00:04:39.212
A lot.

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:42.782
Poppy, the key word here for prosecutors
is intent.

00:04:42.815 --> 00:04:43.349
Right.

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:47.820
How much did Donald Trump know and did
he know that his actions were wrong?

00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:48.955
Now, the new reporting

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:51.391
is that the National Archives,
that's the federal agency

00:04:51.491 --> 00:04:55.228
that's responsible for handling
presidential records during Donald Trump's

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:58.498
presidency, sent him 16
different documents.

00:04:58.531 --> 00:05:00.533
Now, we don't know
exactly what those documents say,

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:04.037
but we do know that they related to
whether why and how

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.173
you should declassify
certain classified records.

00:05:07.173 --> 00:05:10.777
And so what prosecutors are going to argue
is he knew they told him

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.112
this is how you do it
if you want to declassify.

00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:16.516
If he didn't do that, then he did not
declassify.

00:05:16.683 --> 00:05:20.253
Trump has tried to argue before that
these were automatically declassified.

00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:24.123
He did it by his mind
or he argued at one point that he issued

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:27.560
a standing order that any documents I take
are automatically declassify

00:05:27.894 --> 00:05:30.063
We know that's not true
because there's no evidence of it

00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:33.366
and there's no evidence to remember
who circle.

00:05:33.399 --> 00:05:37.603
18 former White House officials
called that claim total nonsense.

00:05:37.603 --> 00:05:38.971
Ridiculous B.S.

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:41.207
a complete fiction and laughable.

00:05:41.207 --> 00:05:43.943
So prosecutors are going to use this
as key evidence of intent.

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:45.912
I was just going to go ahead.

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:48.781
Yes. Also,
if we're looking at the potential charges

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:52.118
here, back when DOJ got permission
to do the search warrant of Mar a Lago,

00:05:52.285 --> 00:05:54.287
they listed three potential crimes.

00:05:54.287 --> 00:05:56.055
One of them was obstruction of justice.

00:05:56.055 --> 00:05:59.092
The other is destroying
or concealing any government document.

00:05:59.092 --> 00:06:00.259
But the third one,

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:03.329
this is the one that matters here,
mishandling defense information.

00:06:03.396 --> 00:06:07.233
There's an argument that that only applies
to classified documents.

00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:11.137
So if Trump did declassify this ones out,
but if he failed to declassify,

00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:12.805
this is still in play for prosecutors.

00:06:12.805 --> 00:06:13.873
What I was going to ask is

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:17.043
you've got to believe Trump's
going to challenge this in the courts.

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:19.512
Is that a tough bar to meet?

00:06:19.846 --> 00:06:21.814
You say you can't see these prosecutors.

00:06:21.814 --> 00:06:23.649
He's very likely to challenge it in court.

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:25.785
He's very unlikely to prevail.

00:06:25.785 --> 00:06:29.188
If he does, he'll argue executive
privilege or say these were communications

00:06:29.188 --> 00:06:32.959
from archives and executive branch
agency over to me when I was president.

00:06:32.959 --> 00:06:34.060
So they're confidential.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.461
It's not that straightforward, though.

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:38.598
We've known for almost 50 years
now, dating back to a famous Supreme

00:06:38.598 --> 00:06:42.168
Court case involving Richard Nixon that,
yes, executive privilege exists,

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:45.505
but it's meant to protect
legitimate strategy, not all yours.

00:06:45.505 --> 00:06:47.340
If there's a possible crime Exactly.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:50.343
Courts have been very disinclined
to use executive privilege

00:06:50.543 --> 00:06:52.512
to block prosecutors
from getting evidence.

00:06:52.512 --> 00:06:56.082
And Trump has tried this and people
around him almost countless times.

00:06:56.082 --> 00:06:59.952
Everyone from Mike Pence, the former vice
president, on down, he has lost

00:07:00.186 --> 00:07:01.521
all of these cases.

00:07:01.521 --> 00:07:05.525
His record is essentially zero
wins on executive privilege, all losses.

00:07:05.525 --> 00:07:08.060
And I think if he challenges this,
he'll have another one in the lawsuit.

00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:09.162
You help us understand

00:07:09.162 --> 00:07:12.632
big picture here, because we all remember
when Trump's attorney general, Bill Barr,

00:07:12.632 --> 00:07:16.202
said in an interview a month or two ago,
essentially the most potentially

00:07:16.202 --> 00:07:20.139
harmful case against the former president
right now is the Mar a Lago Docs case.

00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:23.743
So four pending
potential criminal matters.

00:07:23.743 --> 00:07:25.445
There's a lot swirling around
Donald Trump.

00:07:25.445 --> 00:07:28.981
Now, of course,
he has been indicted by the Manhattan D.A.

00:07:29.182 --> 00:07:33.820
over the falsification allegedly of hush
money payments to Stormy Daniels.

00:07:33.886 --> 00:07:36.422
We also have pending matters down
in Georgia.

00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:37.590
The Fulton County D.A.,

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.993
then the Mar a Lago documents case, which
Bill Barr believes is the biggest threat.

00:07:41.027 --> 00:07:42.562
Hard to sort of weigh them all.

00:07:42.562 --> 00:07:45.765
And then finally,
also an investigation into January six

00:07:45.932 --> 00:07:48.835
and the way this is going to work
within the Justice Department.

00:07:48.835 --> 00:07:50.102
We have Jack Smith.

00:07:50.102 --> 00:07:51.471
He is the special counsel.

00:07:51.471 --> 00:07:53.639
He's in charge of both of these cases,
the Mar

00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:55.608
a Lago documents,
which we were just talking about.

00:07:55.608 --> 00:07:59.078
Plus, January six, he will have
to recommend whether to indict or not.

00:07:59.078 --> 00:08:00.346
But ultimately,

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:03.983
this guy, the attorney general,
he will make the decision, yes or no.

00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:07.019
Ellie Hoenig,
thank you so helpful as always.

